WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.735 --> 00:00:16.985
My name is Delia, and I am a 37-year-old weaver from Chinchero, Peru.

2
00:00:16.986 --> 00:00:24.004
My village is high in the Andes, located over one thousand feet above the Urubamba River.

3
00:00:24.005 --> 00:00:34.449
The view is breathtaking, but what makes Chinchero unique is that here, even though it is now 2008, we are preserving ancient traditions.

4
00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:42.817
In fact, Chinchero is just outside the city of Cuzco, which was once the center of the mighty Inca Empire.

5
00:00:42.818 --> 00:00:45.181
As a traditional master weaver,

6
00:00:45.182 --> 00:00:53.939
I have the privilege and responsibility of teaching my skills to the younger generation, uniting the past with the future.

7
00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:59.912
In fact, I'm waiting for Carla, a neighbor's daughter, to come and take her weaving lesson.

8
00:00:59.913 --> 00:01:05.387
For many women like me, weaving is not just a craft but a way of life.

9
00:01:05.388 --> 00:01:14.205
Peruvian textiles have a rich history that can be traced as far back as 2500 BC, long before the Incas.

10
00:01:14.206 --> 00:01:21.792
By the time of the Inca Empire, in the 1500s, hand-weaving was widely practiced, even by men.

11
00:01:21.793 --> 00:01:33.709
The patterns woven into our textiles have always told stories, so our history was not just expressed orally but was also woven into cloth.

12
00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:43.932
More recently our weaving tradition was sadly being lost as many village people moved to cities, seeking money and a more modern way of life.

13
00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:49.700
But some, like me, have stayed in the village to keep our traditional culture alive.

14
00:01:49.701 --> 00:01:59.159
In 1996 a weaving center was established here, where women, young and old, can come to learn our unique weaving skills.

15
00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:06.003
It is called the Center for Traditional Textiles, and I started to work and teach here in 2000.

16
00:02:06.004 --> 00:02:14.181
The mission of the center is to celebrate the history and traditions of Peruvian textiles and to educate others about them.

17
00:02:14.182 --> 00:02:21.147
After the women weavers gain expertise, their work is sold at the center or at local markets.

18
00:02:21.148 --> 00:02:27.584
This allows the women to gain financial independence or contribute to their family's income.

19
00:02:27.585 --> 00:02:35.556
Practice is a must for good weavers, for we do not rely on fancy, high-speed machines to do the work for us.

20
00:02:35.557 --> 00:02:46.038
Our tools are our fingers—mostly our thumbs—and the yarn we weave comes from either alpaca or vicuna, animals that resemble small llamas.

21
00:02:46.039 --> 00:02:55.412
The alpaca are especially important to our way of life because in addition to their wool, they also supply meat, fertilizer, fuel, and leather.

22
00:02:55.413 --> 00:02:59.822
When I was a child, I often tended our sheep and alpaca.

23
00:02:59.823 --> 00:03:04.110
As I watched them graze, I practiced weaving jakima strips,

24
00:03:04.111 --> 00:03:10.060
small pieces that all the children in my village make to practice different textile patterns.

25
00:03:10.061 --> 00:03:18.508
This practice prepared me to learn more complicated patterns when I was seventeen and began my lessons with an older weaver.

26
00:03:18.509 --> 00:03:23.861
Alpaca and vicuna wool can be many shades of brown, cream, and gray,

27
00:03:23.862 --> 00:03:31.618
but we also dye the wool using natural sources, such as cochineal beetles, which live on prickly pear cactus.

28
00:03:31.619 --> 00:03:37.764
These beetles, when dried and crushed, yield a dye that makes our yarn a brilliant color of red.

29
00:03:37.765 --> 00:03:41.778
We also use eucalyptus leaves and beans to make dyes,

30
00:03:41.779 --> 00:03:47.465
and the vibrant colors from these plants range from pink to different shades of brown and green.

31
00:03:47.466 --> 00:03:54.738
How bright or deep the color is depends on the color of the plant and how long you leave the wool in the dye bath.

32
00:03:54.739 --> 00:04:00.581
Our weavers use a backstrap loom, which is very easy to use and carry around.

33
00:04:00.582 --> 00:04:04.697
Whenever I start a new textile, I make a new loom!

34
00:04:04.698 --> 00:04:11.997
First, I measure out the yarn for the warp, which consists of the threads that will run lengthwise in my textile.

35
00:04:11.998 --> 00:04:18.233
Then, I tie the warp to something immobile, such as a tree or a stake in the ground.

36
00:04:18.234 --> 00:04:26.553
For smaller textile pieces, some women use their own bodies by stretching out a leg and tying the warp to their foot.

37
00:04:26.554 --> 00:04:31.161
The other end of the warp has a strap that is tied around the weaver's waist.

38
00:04:31.162 --> 00:04:37.242
Then, I interlace threads of yarn horizontally across the warp to create the pattern.

39
00:04:37.243 --> 00:04:40.461
The horizontal threads are called the woof.

40
00:04:40.462 --> 00:04:45.914
With all the different colors of yarn, we can make lots of patterns in our textiles.

41
00:04:45.915 --> 00:04:50.968
For example, there is a very special Incan pattern called the eye,

42
00:04:50.969 --> 00:05:00.131
which can be combined with other patterns to represent many things, from the burning of the sun to a gentle whirlpool in a meandering river.

43
00:05:00.132 --> 00:05:09.172
Each textile tells a story about our mountains, rivers, animals, and villages—sometimes even about the weaver herself.

44
00:05:09.173 --> 00:05:15.108
Because patterns and colors are chosen by the weaver to make each textile distinctive,

45
00:05:15.109 --> 00:05:22.934
dark colors may mean the weaver is sad, and bright colors may reflect the happiness she felt as she was weaving.

46
00:05:22.935 --> 00:05:28.718
The most important family textile is the manta, a cape that has many functions—

47
00:05:28.719 --> 00:05:33.962
from hauling fruit and firewood to carrying babies on mothers' backs.

48
00:05:33.963 --> 00:05:37.241
Each village has its own unique manta pattern,

49
00:05:37.242 --> 00:05:43.392
so when others see you, they recognize what village you come from by the colorful design of your manta.

50
00:05:43.393 --> 00:05:50.975
When I weave outside, I sit on a manta, and whatever I am weaving, whether it is a belt, a hat, or a skirt,

51
00:05:50.976 --> 00:05:54.189
I put a lot of time and effort into each piece.

52
00:05:54.190 --> 00:06:02.458
Market day is special for the women of Chinchero, and we like to dress in our traditional handmade clothes when we go to the market.

53
00:06:02.459 --> 00:06:08.768
We wear colorful jackets, braid our hair, and wear our hats, called monteras, proudly.

54
00:06:08.769 --> 00:06:13.328
In our village everyone wears a montera with a round top.

55
00:06:13.329 --> 00:06:20.242
The brim of the hat curves toward its center, and inside the brim is a secret fold where we can store our money.

56
00:06:20.243 --> 00:06:27.547
When a tourist stops by and purchases one of my textiles, I just pop the money into my hat!

57
00:06:27.548 --> 00:06:34.349
The weaving center helps with the sale of our work, but it is up to each weaver to negotiate a price.

58
00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:40.116
To be honest, I don't make a lot of money, only about three hundred dollars a year.

59
00:06:40.117 --> 00:06:44.824
But I am satisfied when I see how my weaving has made someone happy.

60
00:06:44.825 --> 00:06:57.816
Oh, here is Carla! Someday she will make our village proud and weave beautiful textiles that will tell the story of her generation.
